**user**:
> I have a library in python I've written to abstract away from AI chat systems. It provides a function `chat_response(messages, max_tokens=None, model='gpt-5.4-nano')` which I can use to get a response from an AI. `messages` is of the form [{"role": "system", "content": system_message}, {"role": "user", "content": prompt}, ...]. The function is in the file "chatwrap/openaigpt.py".
> 
> Now I'd like to write some code in Julia that uses that system, setting up an initial set of messages (just a system and user message), then taking the response, adding it on as role: assistant message, and then another prompt after that. Can you provide code that does this?
**assistant**:
> ```julia
> using PyCall
> 
> # If chatwrap is not installed in the Python environment used by PyCall:
> # pyimport("sys").path.insert(0, "/path/to/your/python/project")
> 
> openaigpt = pyimport("chatwrap.openaigpt")
> 
> messages = [
>     Dict("role" => "system",
>          "content" => "You are a concise and helpful research assistant."),
>     Dict("role" => "user",
>          "content" => "What is the main purpose of a carbon tax?")
> ]
> 
> # First turn
> response1 = openaigpt.chat_response(
>     messages;
>     model = "gpt-5.4-nano",
>     max_tokens = 300
> )
> 
> println("Assistant: ", response1)
> 
> # Preserve the assistant response in the conversation history
> push!(messages, Dict("role" => "assistant", "content" => String(response1)))
> 
> # Add the next user turn
> push!(messages, Dict(
>     "role" => "user",
>     "content" => "Give one important limitation or political challenge."
> ))
> 
> # Second turn, with full prior context
> response2 = openaigpt.chat_response(
>     messages;
>     model = "gpt-5.4-nano",
>     max_tokens = 300
> )
> 
> println("Assistant: ", response2)
> ```
> 
> Make sure `PyCall` is configured to use the same Python environment where `chatwrap` and its dependencies are available.
